          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %em Cartan-Eilenberg categories
            is a tool in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+algebra"} homotopical algebra
          %p
            The structure of a
            %em Cartan-Eilenberg category
            (
            %a{:href => "#SAPR"}> SAPR 10
            ) on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            is the structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
            equipped with the further data of “strong” equivalences inducing a notion of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrant+objects"} cofibrant objects
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
            of the category at the weak equivalences is equivalently that of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
            of cofibrant objects at just the strong equivalences.
          %p
            The motivation for this axiomatics in (
            %a{:href => "#SAPR"}> SAPR 10
            ) is that it is the general context in which the original construction in (
            %a{:href => "#CartanEilenberg56"}> Cartan-Eilenberg 56
            ) of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functors"} derived functors
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+functors"} additive functors
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+modules"} categories of modules
            generalizes.
          %p
            Every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            is in particular a Cartan-Eilenberg category, the strong equivalences in this case are the left
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalences"} homotopy equivalences
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+with+weak+equivalences"} categories with weak equivalences
            and fully-fledged model categories, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+fibrant+objects"} categories of fibrant objects
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Waldhausen+categories"} Waldhausen categories
            etc.
          %p
            In this approach to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+algebra"} homotopical algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒲
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{W}
            of distinguished
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            called the
            %em strong
            and the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
            and with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S\subseteq W
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            of the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            is said to be
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrant+object"} cofibrant
            if any morphism from it to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain"} codomain
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalence"} weak equivalence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lifting+property"} lifts
            uniquely to one from it to the domain within the category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒮
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}[\mathcal{S}^{-1}]
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} inverting
            the
            %em strong
            equivalences. (Note as the context is slightly different
            %em cofibrant
            means something slightly different here.)
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A triple
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝒲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{C},\mathcal{S},\mathcal{W})
            is called a (left)
            %em Cartan-Eilenberg category
            if every object has a cofibrant left model that is, for each object,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo :
                  %mi M
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q: M\to X
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒮
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}[\mathcal{S}^{-1}]
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒲
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}[\mathcal{W}^{-1}]
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            These Cartan-Eilenberg categories have the property that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒲
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}[\mathcal{W}^{-1}]
            is equivalent to a relative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localisation"} localisation
            of the category of cofibrant objects with respect to strong equivalences.
          %h2#references References
          %p The original constructions from which “Cartan-Eilenberg categories” got their name are due to
          %ul#CartanEilenberg56
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Henri+Cartan"}> Henri Cartan
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samuel+Eilenberg"} Samuel Eilenberg
              = succeed "." do
                %em Homological Algebra
          %p
            The abstraction of
            %em Cartan-Eilenberg categories
            then appears in
          %ul#SAPR
            %li
              F. Guillén Santos, Vicente Navarro Aznar, Pere Pascual, Agusti Roig,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A Cartan-Eilenberg approach to Homotopical Algebra
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0707.3704"} arxiv/0707.3704
              and J. Pure Applied. Alg. 214 (2010) 140 - 164.
          %p Further discussion is in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Pere Pascual,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Some remarks on Cartan-Eilenberg categories
                %a{:href => "http://upcommons.upc.edu/e-prints/bitstream/2117/9290/1/prepr201001pascual.pdf"} pdf
                and Collect. Math. 63, No. 2, 203-216 (2012);
            %li
              %p
                Pere Pascual,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Cartan-Eilenberg categories and descent categories
                %a{:href => "http://www.ma1.upc.edu/recerca/reports-recerca/repre-2009/Fitxers/rep200902pascual.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                Joana Cirici, Francisco Guillén,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Homotopy theory of mixed Hodge complexes
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1304.6236"} arxiv/1304.6236
